<p>
  An LCR meter measures inductance (L), capacitance (C), and resistance (R) — and, on better instruments, the secondary parameters that reveal a component's real-world condition, not just its nominal value. Choosing the right one means matching test frequency, accuracy, and measurement range to the components you actually work with. This guide covers the specifications that separate a basic component checker from a precision LCR meter.
</p>

<h2 id="test-frequency">Test Frequency — Why It Changes the Reading</h2>

<p>
  A component's measured inductance or capacitance is not a fixed physical constant — it varies with the frequency at which it's tested, because real components have parasitic effects (winding capacitance in an inductor, equivalent series inductance in a capacitor) that become significant at different frequencies. This is why an LCR meter always specifies the test frequency alongside its reading, and why comparing two measurements taken at different test frequencies is not valid. Most general-purpose LCR meters test at a fixed 100 Hz, 120 Hz, 1 kHz, or 10 kHz; higher-end models let you select the test frequency to match the component's actual operating frequency in-circuit.
</p>

<Callout type="info" title="Always test at (or near) the component's working frequency">
  A filter capacitor destined for a 100 kHz switch-mode supply will not show its true in-circuit behaviour when measured at 120 Hz — its ESR and effective capacitance both shift with frequency. For power electronics and RF work, an LCR meter with selectable test frequency gives a far more representative reading than a fixed-frequency instrument.
</Callout>

<h2 id="secondary-parameters">Secondary Parameters — ESR, D, and Q</h2>

<p>Beyond the primary L, C, or R reading, LCR meters report secondary parameters that reveal how close a component is to ideal behaviour:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>ESR (Equivalent Series Resistance)</strong>: The resistive loss inside a capacitor. A capacitor with a normal capacitance reading but abnormally high ESR is a classic sign of an ageing or drying-out electrolytic capacitor — one of the most common real-world component failures, and one a basic capacitance-only tester will completely miss.</li>
  <li><strong>D (Dissipation Factor)</strong>: The ratio of a capacitor's resistive loss to its reactive (ideal) behaviour — lower is better, and a rising D value over a component's life is an early warning of degradation.</li>
  <li><strong>Q (Quality Factor)</strong>: The inverse concept for inductors — the ratio of an inductor's reactance to its internal resistance, higher is better. Q is central to evaluating inductors and coils for RF and resonant circuit work.</li>
</ul>

