<h2 id="measurement-range">Measurement Range</h2>

<p>
  Confirm the meter's range covers both ends of what you actually work with — small-signal capacitors down to picofarads and inductors down to microhenries need meaningfully different sensitivity than large electrolytic capacitors in the thousands of microfarads or power inductors in the millihenry range. A meter optimised for small precision components may not read large electrolytic capacitors accurately, and vice versa.
</p>

<h2 id="test-leads">Test Lead Configuration</h2>

<p>
  Precision LCR measurement uses a 4-terminal (Kelvin) connection — separate current-carrying and voltage-sensing leads — to eliminate the resistance of the test leads themselves from the measurement. This matters most for low-value resistance and ESR readings, where lead resistance (a few tens of milliohms) can be a significant fraction of the value being measured. A simple 2-terminal connection is adequate for general L/C measurement where lead resistance is negligible relative to the reading.
</p>

<h2 id="buying-summary">Quick Reference by Use Case</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>General repair / component sorting</strong>: Fixed frequency, basic L/C/R, auto-ranging.</li>
  <li><strong>Capacitor health diagnostics</strong>: ESR measurement is the priority — look for a meter with dedicated in-circuit or out-of-circuit ESR mode.</li>
  <li><strong>Power electronics / magnetics design</strong>: Selectable test frequency matching actual switching frequency, Q measurement for inductor/transformer design.</li>
  <li><strong>Incoming inspection / QC</strong>: Higher accuracy class, 4-terminal test leads, repeatable fixture-based measurement.</li>
</ul>

